text:
I&#x27;m <i>only</i> talking about modern C++, not C++98. C++&#x27;s footguns include smart pointers. For example, is the following code safe?<p><pre><code>    std::unique_ptr&lt;int&gt; p(foo());
    std::print(&quot;{}\n&quot;, *p);
</code></pre>
The answer depends on how foo() is implemented. You can&#x27;t reason about it locally, and strictly speaking it&#x27;s not possible to determine at compile time. Now, I should be declaring this gsl::not_null&lt;std::unique_ptr&lt;int&gt;&gt; to comply with the core guidelines, but I think we can agree that optional support libraries aren&#x27;t relevant to this discussion.
